EXPERIMENT TASK — CONSTRUCT MUSIQUE QUESTION GRAPH

We are constructing a grounded RDF graph for one MuSiQue benchmark question.

The question, expected answer, and decomposition are coordination context. They
help keep entity and predicate choices compatible across supporting paragraphs.
They are not evidence.

For the CURRENT SOURCE only, return grounded RDF statements using the extraction
schema below. Reuse semantic names already present in the accepted graph when
they denote the same thing.
Extract all useful explicit atomic facts from CURRENT SOURCE.

Do not restrict extraction to facts needed for the question.

However, facts explicitly present in CURRENT SOURCE that are required
to realize its MuSiQue decomposition hop(s) are mandatory and must not
be omitted.

Question/answer/decomposition/accepted graph are coordination context,
not evidence.

Every returned statement must be supported by the CURRENT SOURCE. Python owns
source identity; never return source IDs.

Question: Who married the man who wanted to reform the institution behind the denomination constituting 12.6% of Christians in New Zealand?

Expected answer: Katharina von Bora

Gold decomposition:
1. question='What Christina denomination makes up 12.6% of Christians?'; answer='Roman Catholicism'; support_paragraph=13
2. question='#1 in Lesotho >> part of'; answer='Catholic Church'; support_paragraph=2
3. question='who wanted #2 to reform and address'; answer='Martin Luther'; support_paragraph=17
4. question='#3 >> spouse'; answer='Katharina von Bora'; support_paragraph=9

Accepted semantic graph so far:
(empty)

CURRENT SOURCE — MuSiQue paragraph 13

{
  "title": "New Zealand",
  "text": "Christianity is the predominant religion in New Zealand, although its society is among the most secular in the world. In the 2013 census, 55.0% of the population identified with one or more religions, including 49.0% identifying as Christians. Another 41.9% indicated that they had no religion. The main Christian denominations are, by number of adherents, Roman Catholicism (12.6%), Anglicanism (11.8%), Presbyterianism (8.5%) and \"Christian not further defined\" (i.e. people identifying as Christian but not stating the denomination, 5.5%). The Māori-based Ringatū and Rātana religions (1.4%) are also Christian in origin. Immigration and demographic change in recent decades has contributed to the growth of minority religions, such as Hinduism (2.1%), Buddhism (1.5%), Islam (1.2%) and Sikhism (0.5%). The Auckland Region exhibited the greatest religious diversity."
}

